From 8641f099f4bee797ecb2c5799cac4ac9fff77bb5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter Laux <hunterlaux@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 22:30:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 29/29] mips: jz47xx: x2600: DDR parameters by the SoC ID in
 the eFuse

The X2600 comes in variants with different DDR dies in the package:
the AD5X ships with the X2600H (512 MiB DDR3L) and the X2600E (256 MiB
DDR3). The SPL always set up the X2600H's DDR, so on an X2600E its DDR
test failed.

Read the SoC info from the eFuse as Ingenic's check_socid() does, build
the DDR ID from it and pick the matching table: the X2600H's as before,
the X2600E's as the 2025 stock SPL carries it.

With 256 MiB, leave the second bank (mem=256M@0x30000000) off the
kernel command line, in the SPL and in U-Boot proper, as the stock SPL
does.

Signed-off-by: Hunter Laux <hunterlaux@gmail.com>
[Kiln OS: one decision for the SPL and U-Boot proper in
 mach/x2600_ram.h, plain C for the laptop tests. Fail-safe: an X2600H,
 a blank eFuse, a read that doesn't finish, check bits that differ or
 a variant Ingenic doesn't list keep the X2600H's table, as before;
 only a good SoC ID that isn't an X2600H and decodes to DDR3 with
 256 MiB picks the X2600E's. The X2600N's 128 MiB table dropped: the
 kernels' memory layout needs the low 256 MiB, so any other DDR stops
 the attempt before the DDR setup (spl_fail, then the boot ROM's USB
 mode after the last one). The X2600E's table is static const and
 copied over the X2600H's, so the DDR code is unchanged. The eFuse is
 powered down after the read, as check_socid() does. The second bank
 is dropped as any word mem=...@0x30000000, wherever it is. The SPL
 logs the SoC ID and the table it chose. The DDR test is unchanged:
 address lines 2-27 are the low 256 MiB on both.]
---
